<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">HMS Beagle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">HMS Beagle was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> brig-sloop launched in 1820 and converted for survey work. On her second voyage, from 1831 to 1836 under Captain Robert FitzRoy, she surveyed the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and sailed round the world, carrying the young naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>, whose observations, including those in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a>, shaped his theory of evolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hms-beagle.jpg" alt="HMS Beagle" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R. T. Pritchett · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V57_D097_Hms_beagle_in_the_straits_of_magellan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>HMS Beagle was a Cherokee-class 10-gun brig-sloop, one of more than 100 ships of a class designed by Sir Henry Peake in 1807. Her keel was laid in June 1818 and she was launched from Woolwich Dockyard on the Thames on 11 May 1820, at a cost of £7,803. Later reports say that she took part in the celebrations of the coronation of George IV, passing under the old London Bridge. With no immediate need for her, she lay in ordinary, moored without masts or rigging. In 1825 she was fitted out for survey work in the Hydrographic Office. Her guns were reduced from ten to six and a mizzen mast was added, which changed her from a brig to a barque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>On her first voyage, which left Plymouth on 22 May 1826 under Pringle Stokes, she accompanied the larger HMS Adventure on a survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a>. Stokes fell into a deep depression and shot himself at Port Famine in 1828, dying on 12 August. Robert FitzRoy, then 23, took command and proved an able surveyor. During this survey the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beagle-channel">Beagle Channel</a> was identified and named after the ship.</p><p>FitzRoy was reappointed on 27 June 1831 for a second voyage and had the ship rebuilt at Devonport with a raised upper deck. She carried 22 chronometers and was one of the first ships fitted with William Snow Harris's lightning conductor. FitzRoy wanted a naturalist for company and for the study of the land, and the search led to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>. The ship left Devonport on 10 December 1831 and, after surveying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, returned by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> and Hobart Town to Falmouth on 2 October 1836.</p><p>A third voyage, from 1837 to 1843 under John Clements Wickham and then John Lort Stokes, surveyed the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. It explored the Gulf of Carpentaria, which revealed the Albert and Flinders rivers, and on 9 October 1839 Wickham named Port Darwin in honour of their former shipmate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Darwin combined his diary with his scientific notes in Journal and Remarks, published in 1839 as the third volume of the official account of the expedition. It was widely popular and was reprinted many times as The Voyage of the Beagle. His findings were decisive in the formation of his theories of evolution and natural selection. In 1845 the ship was renamed WV-7 and refitted as a static coastguard watch vessel stationed on the Essex coast to control smuggling.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “HMS Beagle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Galápagos Islands, visited by Charles Darwin aboard <b>HMS Beagle</b> in 1835, supplied observations that later shaped his theory of evolution by natural selection, and in 1978 the islands and the historic centre of Quito were among the first sites inscribed on UNESCO’s World Heritage List.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>HMS Beagle</b> left Plymouth on a surveying voyage with the 22-year-old naturalist Charles Darwin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-27">27 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Born on the same day as Lincoln, Darwin sailed on <b>HMS Beagle</b> and developed the theory of evolution by natural selection, published in 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-12">12 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
